  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-3X+4 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-3X+4I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(B)={B}^{2}-3B+4I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${B}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(B):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B) & ={B}^{2}-3B+4I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-3+4 & -3-(-3)+0 \\\\ -9-(-9)+0 & 7-6+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
